From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: dp: link training -- CR and EQ against drm_dp_helper, with fallback

Link training was the last piece of the pixel path that was still done by hand.
As of 2026-08-19 the clocks, TCON_TV1 and the eDP video stream are driver code
and HW-proven -- a 1920x1200 picture came up from
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`,
`arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`,
`drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` and
`drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` alone -- with training done by hand over AUX
in between.

What the DRM core does, and what it does not
--------------------------------------------

drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_dp_helper.c provides predicates and delays --
drm_dp_clock_recovery_ok(), drm_dp_channel_eq_ok(),
drm_dp_get_adjust_request_voltage/pre_emphasis(),
drm_dp_dpcd_read_link_status(), drm_dp_link_train_clock_recovery_delay(),
drm_dp_link_train_channel_eq_delay(), drm_dp_read_*_delay(), the LTTPR helpers,
drm_dp_link_rate_to_bw_code(), drm_dp_max_link_rate/lane_count(),
drm_dp_tps3_supported(), drm_dp_enhanced_frame_cap().

It does not provide a state machine. There is no generic drm_dp_link_train().
Fourteen drivers in 6.18.36 each write their own CR/EQ loop, and the "five
attempts at an unchanged voltage swing" rule of DP 1.4 section 3.5.1.2.2 is
implemented nine times in nine different ways -- the number 5 does not appear in
a single core header (grep MAX_TRAINING_TRIES/voltage_tries include/drm/ returns
nothing).

A drm_dp_link_train() does exist, at drivers/gpu/drm/tegra/dp.c:792, together
with a whole drm_dp_link_* family. It is Tegra-private -- no EXPORT_SYMBOL in
that file -- so it is not callable from here.

Every helper the core has is called here and none is open-coded. The loop shape
follows zynqmp_dp_link_train() (drivers/gpu/drm/xlnx/zynqmp_dp.c:694-1010) and
the fallback ladder follows hibmc_dp_link_training()
(drivers/gpu/drm/hisilicon/hibmc/dp/dp_link.c:310-420) -- both use the core
predicates throughout.

Nothing upstream targets Allwinner DP
-------------------------------------

drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/ has no DisplayPort at all (its only "edp" hits are a
reset-line name in sun4i_tcon.c:1145,1151), there is no allwinner DP/eDP binding
in Documentation/devicetree/bindings/display/, and every allwinner PHY binding
is USB or MIPI-DPHY. dw-dp.c is a Synopsys DesignWare library with exactly one
consumer, dw_dp-rockchip.c, bound to "rockchip,rk3588-dp" -- a different IP from
the Trilinear core at 0x05740000, so its registers do not apply here. Its loop is also unbounded in CR, has no lane fallback, and leaves a union
phy_configure_opts uninitialised on the stack (dw-dp.c:559).

What this patch does, with the vendor driver for reference
----------------------------------------------------------

The vendor sequence is used as a reference; no code is taken from it.

  - Rate ladder, then lane ladder, DP 1.4 section 3.5.1.2.1. See "What this
    cannot do today" below. (The vendor driver has no rate or lane fallback.)
  - Ten CR rounds, five at an unchanged request, and a separate max-swing exit,
    as DP 1.4 section 3.5.1.2.2 requires. (The vendor makes one attempt at
    maximum swing.)
  - The link-status read is checked at every read; a NACK/DEFER/timeout is
    reported as itself and aborts the attempt. (In the vendor driver
    edp_get_link_status()'s return is not checked at both call sites, over a
    buffer zeroed first, so a dead AUX channel looks like "CR not done".)
  - The sink is asked again once the training pattern is cleared and the
    scrambler is on, and success is its answer, not the loop's. See the section
    below.
  - CR_DONE is re-checked every EQ round, DP 1.4 section 3.5.1.2.3, and returned
    as a different errno (-ENOLINK) from "the rounds ran out with CR intact"
    (-EPROTO), because the ladder has to tell them apart. (The vendor's EQ does
    not re-check CR_DONE.)
  - DPCD 0x107 DOWNSPREAD_CTRL and 0x108 MAIN_LINK_CHANNEL_CODING_SET are both
    written. 0x107 is written zero on purpose: this PHY has no SSC arm and
    refuses phy_configure() with ssc set, so promising the sink a spread clock
    would describe a source that does not exist. (The vendor writes neither.)
  - TR_PHY_STATUS is read and logged before TPS1, and not gated on; see below.
    (The vendor declares it and does not read it.)
  - The per-lane swing/pre-emphasis index is clamped to DP 1.4 Table 3-1 here
    and refused in the PHY (`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis`).
    (The vendor takes an unbounded per-lane index from sysfs into
    dp_lv_table[sw][pre].)
  - The two adjust bytes are used per lane: DPCD 0x206 carries lanes 0/1 and
    0x207 lanes 2/3, and on this board they differ. (The vendor folds them into
    one value.)

TR_PHY_STATUS
-------------

It is PHY_STATUS at 0x0280, UM 15.4.7.38, and the UM defines no bits -- the
field only defers to a note about whichever PHY is actually used. There is no
such application note in this tree. So a "PHY ready" predicate built on any bit
of it would be an invented predicate, and none is built. It is read and printed
at dev_dbg immediately before TPS1 and in the `train` file, so the value is on
record; nothing branches on it.

ENHANCED_FRAME_EN
-----------------

ENHANCED_FRAME_EN (0x0008) is read-only: UM 15.4.7.3 describes it as hard-wired
to 1. So the source is in enhanced framing whatever anyone writes, DPCD 0x101
bit 7 is the source describing itself rather than choosing, and a sink without
ENHANCED_FRAME_CAP cannot be framed correctly by this transmitter at all. That
gets one dev_warn naming both sides rather than a silent mismatch.

Why the sink is asked again after training
------------------------------------------

A board run on 2026-08-19, on a fixture that passed the acceptance script's
alt-mode preflight: `echo 0 > .../train` returned exit 0 and a version of this
driver that judged CR and EQ from link status taken during training reported:

    state         : trained at 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes     <- the driver
    attempts      : 1 (CR 1 round(s), EQ 3)

while the sink's own bytes, read through the same file, said:

    DPCD 0x202-207: 00 00 80 00 00 00                     <- the sink
    clock recovery: NOT done
    equalisation  : NOT done
    sink in sync  : no
    symbol errors : lane 0: 329, lanes 1/2/3: 32767 (saturated)

CR and EQ had been judged from link status taken during training and not asked
again once the training pattern was cleared and the scrambler enabled.

sun60i_dp_train_verify() answers that. It runs after sun60i_dp_train_stop() and
before success is returned. It reads DPCD 0x202..0x207 once with
drm_dp_dpcd_read_link_status() and requires drm_dp_clock_recovery_ok() and
drm_dp_channel_eq_ok() -- i.e. CR_DONE on every active lane, CHANNEL_EQ_DONE and
SYMBOL_LOCKED on every active lane, and INTERLANE_ALIGN_DONE. Both predicates
are the core's; neither is open-coded. That is the by-hand final check
(LANE0_1=0x77 LANE2_3=0x77 ALIGN=0x01) in the core's terms.

One read, no retry. A second look after a failed first one would turn "this link
does not hold once training stops" into "it held the second time", which is the
question, not noise to average away.

`train`'s `state` line is built from that reading instead of from dp->trained.
The driver's own flag is still printed -- it is what tells an A/B run apart from
a link trained by hand -- but under its own label, below the sink's verdict, and
labelled as a claim.

Missing check, or enable order?
-------------------------------

"The link loses lock the moment scrambling is enabled" is the kind of symptom
that gets a retry bolted onto it, so the cause was checked. It is not the
ordering inside sun60i_dp_train_stop(). That sequence -- source
TRAINING_PATTERN_SET to 0, settle, DPCD 0x102 to 0, then source
SCRAMBLING_DISABLE to 0 -- is step for step what the by-hand sequence does, on
the runs that produced 77 77 01 01 and a picture (the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-18
and 2026-08-19). It stays as it is.

What the driver omits is the step taken immediately afterwards by the by-hand
sequence: enabling the video stream. `drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` (HW-
proven) records that with training cleared and the stream not enabled the core
sends idle and the sink loses the lock it just gained, as happened on the first
successful training, 2026-08-18. `echo 0 > train` ends in precisely that state.
So the sink's 00 00 80 00 00 00 is the fingerprint of a missing check and also
the resting state of an unfinished sequence; a check that ignored that would
replace one wrong verdict with the opposite one.

Which is why SINK=0x01 -- the fourth number of the by-hand check -- is not
required by sun60i_dp_train_verify(). DPCD 0x205 RECEIVE_PORT_0_STATUS describes
the sink's synchronisation to a received stream, not the outcome of training.
Mainline polls it for 1 only after the video stream starts and back to 0 after
it stops -- edp_receiver_ready_T7() and _T9(), the eDP stream-on / stream-off
timings, drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/protocols/link_edp_panel_control.c
:480 and :455 -- and no DisplayPort driver in 6.18.36 gates training on it; the
only other readers of 0x205 take the DP 2.0 INTRA_HOP bit
(drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dp_link_training.c:1109,
.../link/protocols/link_dp_training.c:1037). i915 says the rest at
intel_dp_link_training.c:1127: what a source transmits after training "is
platform specific ... on older platforms it's HW specific if/how an idle pattern
is generated".

So the bit is required where it is defined, which is two places:

  - in sun60i_dp_train_verify() itself when a stream is already running
    (dp->video_on) -- the re-training case, which is what a KMS driver will do on
    an IRQ_HPD link-status change. There the fourth number is meaningful at the
    end of training, and it is required.
  - in sun60i_dp_video_stream_enable(), which waits for it, bounded, and does not
    report the stream started until the sink says it is receiving it.

Bounded polling, not a retry -- nothing is re-driven between reads, and the wait
is the eDP T7 allowance mainline polls for in the same shape (the by-hand
sequence waits 500 ms before it reads). The stream is left enabled on failure,
because the next thing done with a black screen is to read the registers.

The bit is not asked for in the first training of a link that has no stream
behind it yet; that is skipped with a dev_dbg line saying so.

Open: whether the link bits (CR / EQ / symbol lock / interlane align) hold on
this core's idle at all. If they do not, sun60i_dp_train_verify() will refuse a
link that trained correctly, and the answer is that `train` and the stream
cannot be separate verbs on this part. That outcome is checkable and the checks
below ask for it directly.

Open: between the DPCD 0x102 write and the source's SCRAMBLING_DISABLE write,
the sink descrambles a source that is still sending unscrambled output -- one
AUX transaction wide. The by-hand sequence has the same window and works.
Check: symbol-error counters that are non-zero at the end of a link that
otherwise reads 0x77/0x77/0x01/0x01.

What this cannot do today
-------------------------

The rate ladder is one rung long. The PHY has exactly one PMA rate table, 2700
Mb/s (`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`); every other rung is refused
by phy_validate() and never entered. So rate fallback is plumbing waiting for a
second table -- only the lane fallback (4 -> 2 -> 1) can change anything today.
A second rate would need a second 163-word PMA table in the PHY, its own
reset-bracket run, and its own swing table; none of that is here.

Rate before lanes, which keeps bandwidth: four lanes at a lower rate carry more
than two lanes at the same rate. The exception is an EQ-only failure -- clock
recovery proved out, so the clock is fine and the problem is inter-lane, which
fewer lanes can fix and a lower rate need not. That case skips the rest of the
rate ladder and drops the lane count, which is why train_eq() returns -EPROTO
there and -ENOLINK when CR was lost. hibmc_dp_link_training() makes the same
distinction (dp_link.c:310-338).

The rungs are rebuilt when the lane count drops, because a PHY may allow a rate
at two lanes that it refuses at four -- and phy_validate() is asked before each
attempt rather than after tearing a working link down to find out. (The vendor
maps both -EOPNOTSUPP and -EINVAL from phy_validate() to "pass",
sunxi_drm_edp.c:846-868.)

The interface
-------------

A `train` debugfs file beside `link` and `video`, for the same reason those two
are debugfs: this tree has no KMS driver and no HPD path into one, so there is
nothing else to call sun60i_dp_train(). Writing "<rate Mb/s> [lanes]" trains
exactly that; writing "0" walks the ladder. Reading it asks the sink -- the
`state` line included, and everything below it, all of it back over AUX from
DPCD 0x202..0x207 and 0x210.., not from a register of ours reading back.

    state         : sink confirms the link at 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes
    driver claim  : trained (this driver's own flag; not evidence)

Two lines, in that order. They agree on a healthy link and they are the two
halves of the A/B: a link the sink confirms while the driver does not claim it
was trained by hand.

The symbol-error counters are printed with their VALID bit, not just their
count: bit 15 clear means the sink is not counting, which is not the same as
zero errors.

sun60i_dp_video_stream_enable() warns when the link was not trained by this
driver rather than refusing. That is deliberate and temporary: training by hand
is still the A/B arm until this patch is HW-proven, and refusing would make the
two impossible to compare on one kernel. The warning tells the two runs apart in
a log.

Checks
------

Every item is a reading on the 8 GiB board with a DP sink on J4, on a kernel
carrying `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-lane-map-and-inverts` +
`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis` + this patch.

  0. The fixture is alive. This is a gate rather than a check because on
     2026-08-19 it was not: a Type-C recovery had left DP alt mode, the four
     COMB0 lanes were in reset, and a full page of failures came out of a dead
     fixture. A DPCD read succeeding does not establish alt mode -- AUX runs over
     the SBU pair through its own PHY (`phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`) and
     answers with the main link in reset. Asking TCPM alone
     (`port0-partner.0/active`) answers "did the partner enter an alt mode", not
     "are the four COMB0 lanes carrying DisplayPort". Three witnesses are
     required: TCPM's `active` = yes, the combo-PHY's own handover line in dmesg
     ("DP alt mode pin assignment <C|E>: handing all four COMB0 lanes to
     DisplayPort", with no later "DP alt mode left"), and `dp_active : 1` in the
     combo-PHY's debugfs `state`. Fails if: any of the three is missing -- then
     nothing below means anything.
     Recovery is a power cycle. The unbind/bind of the rt1711h i2c device is not
     a safe recovery: its second use on 2026-08-19 left DP alt mode ("DP alt mode
     left, COMB0 links back in reset") and the kernel then wedged with RCU stalls
     on one CPU until the board was power-cycled anyway.

  1. The link trains from the driver, and the sink agrees.
     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train
     Expected: exit 0, dmesg "link trained: 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes, CR in 1
     round(s), EQ in 1", and `cat train` showing "state         : sink confirms
     the link", "driver claim  : trained", clock recovery done, equalisation
     done, four symbol-error counters valid at 0. "sink in sync" reads NO here
     and that is correct -- it is a stream bit; item 3 is where it counts.
     Fails if: a non-zero exit; "PHY refused the drive levels"
     (`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis` not in the kernel);
     "the sink does not confirm ... once the training pattern is cleared"; CR
     completing only after several rounds where training by hand does it in one
     on the same cable and monitor.

  2. The drive levels reached the PMA. TX_TXCC_MGNFS_MULT_000, lanes 0..3, at
     0x06C880A0 / 0x06C884A0 / 0x06C888A0 / 0x06C88CA0 -- that is
     0x06C80000 + ((0x4000 + lane*0x200 + 0x50) << 1), the address
     a733_combophy_dp_set_voltages() writes and the one the rate table of
     `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700` names as
     0x4050/0x4250/0x4450/0x4650.
     Expected: each inside 0x0009..0x0028, the band every legal non-maximum cell
     of the table in `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis`
     occupies, with both 16-bit halves equal.
     Fails if: 0x002A, which is what the rate table leaves, i.e. set_voltages
     never ran; 0x0000, which is either no write at all or a link resting at
     maximum drive; anything outside the band, which no cell produces.

  3. A picture, from the drivers only, and the sink says it is receiving it.
     echo configure > .../video ; echo 0 > .../train ; echo stream > .../video
     Expected: `echo stream` exits 0 -- which means the sink reported
     RECEIVE_PORT_0_STATUS within the bounded wait -- a 1920x1200 test pattern on
     the screen, `cat train` showing "sink in sync  : yes" and still "state
     : sink confirms the link", and no "the main link was not trained by this
     driver" line in dmesg.
     Fails if: "the sink does not report itself in sync with the stream after N
     ms"; a black screen with every register right, which is what happened on
     2026-08-18 and is why nothing here replaces the eye.

  4. Training by hand is no longer needed -- the A/B arm proves it. Same kernel,
     same board, once with the driver's `train` and once with training by hand
     given 4 lanes and 2700: both must give 77 77 01 01.
     Fails if: training by hand trains a link the driver cannot.

  5. Lane fallback, the only ladder arm that can move today. Force it:
     echo "2700 4" > train on a two-lane pin assignment, or unplug two lanes.
     Expected: the attempt at 4 fails, the driver retries at 2, and `train`
     reports 2 lanes with attempts >= 2.
     Fails if: the driver stops at the first failure.

  6. The five-tries rule bounds. With no sink able to lock (a deliberately bad
     cable), training must return in bounded time and log "attempts at an
     unchanged drive level" or "every lane at maximum swing", not hang.
     Fails if: an unbounded loop, or a single attempt.

  7. Held. Items 1 and 3 unchanged over minutes, not sampled once.

  8. Open question: do the link bits hold on this core's idle? Item 1 answers it
     either way. If `train` alone reports "the sink does not confirm ... once the
     training pattern is cleared" while item 3 then produces a picture and
     77 77 01 01, the answer is no -- the core's idle does not hold the sink --
     and `train` and `stream` cannot be separate verbs on this part. That is a
     design change to make with the measurement in hand, not a retry to add now.

Not evidence: a green build, the `train` file existing, or the driver's own
`trained` flag -- which is why every number the file prints, `state` included,
comes from the sink.

Hardware status
---------------

  `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-lane-map-and-inverts`  HW-proven, the 8 GiB
        board, 2026-08-19. Lane map 0x00003210 at 0x06C06C24, lane invert 0xf,
        with the test instrumentation never touched (`forced: no`), so the values
        reach the production path from the mux callback.
  `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis`  not proven, in either
        direction. Nothing in that session exercised it.
  `drm-a733-dp-link-training`  not proven. Its first version reported a link the
        sink denied, on a fixture whose alt-mode preflight had passed; that is
        fixed above.

Whether the CR/EQ loop can train a healthy link is neither proven nor refuted.
The run answered "does the driver tell the truth about the link" and left "can
this loop train a link" open: the attempts made after the Type-C recovery could
not answer it, because that recovery had left DP alt mode ("DP alt mode left,
COMB0 links back in reset") and the lanes were in reset. The same failure
reproduced on a kernel without these patches, which rules the patches out as its
cause.

Build check: the series applies at --fuzz=0 on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36 tree,
drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ builds clean at W=1, checkpatch 0/0.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -14,9 +14,10 @@
  * The main link is the second, and it is a PHY consumer's job: COMB0 belongs to
  * the combo-PHY driver, and a DisplayPort controller reaches it through
  * phy_configure() with a union phy_configure_opts_dp, the way every DP
- * controller in mainline does. What is still absent is link training and video:
- * no training pattern sequence, no DPCD 0x100/0x102 writes, no mode setting, no
- * KMS.
+ * controller in mainline does. Link training is the third, and with it the last
+ * piece of the pixel path that was still a shell script. What is still absent
+ * is KMS: no connector, no CRTC, no modeset -- the mode is a built-in constant
+ * and the verbs are debugfs files.
  *
  * Every register offset and field below comes from the UM's own register list
  * (chapter 15.4.7 of the A733 user manual) and
@@ -64,6 +65,7 @@
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 #include <linux/sprintf.h>
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
+#include <linux/unaligned.h>
 
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h>
@@ -141,6 +143,7 @@
 static const unsigned int sun60i_dp_link_rates[] = { 810000, 540000, 270000, 162000 };
 
 #define SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(r)	((unsigned int)(r) / 100)
+#define SUN60I_DP_MBPS_TO_RATE(m)	((int)(m) * 100)
 
 /*
  * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -296,6 +299,16 @@ struct sun60i_dp {
 	bool			dpcd_valid;
 	unsigned int		link_rate;
 	unsigned int		link_lanes;
+	/* Link training. `trained` is this driver's own answer and therefore not
+	 * evidence -- the sink's answer is DPCD 0x202..0x205 and the `train`
+	 * file reads it every time it is opened. The three counters exist
+	 * because "it trained" and "it trained on the first attempt" are
+	 * different results and only the second one is a healthy link.
+	 */
+	bool			trained;
+	unsigned int		train_attempts;
+	unsigned int		train_cr_rounds;
+	unsigned int		train_eq_rounds;
 	/* The main video stream. The second reg window is eDP_REG_PAD at
 	 * 0x05760000, which the AUX path measurably never needs and video
 	 * cannot do without; a DT with one window still gets a working AUX
@@ -899,6 +912,78 @@ static int sun60i_dp_video_configure(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The sink's own answer about the stream it is receiving.
+ *
+ * DPCD 0x205 bit 0 RECEIVE_PORT_0_STATUS, and the link bits underneath it. This
+ * is the point at which all four numbers of the by-hand final check are
+ * defined at once -- LANE0_1=0x77 LANE2_3=0x77 ALIGN=0x01 SINK=0x01 --
+ * and the only point, because SINK is a
+ * STREAM bit: mainline polls it for 1 only once the stream has started
+ * (edp_receiver_ready_T7(),
+ * drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/protocols/link_edp_panel_control.c:480)
+ * and back to 0 when it stops (_T9(), :455). sun60i_dp_train_verify() therefore
+ * requires the other three and this requires all four.
+ *
+ * Bounded POLLING, which is not a retry. Nothing is re-driven between reads:
+ * the sink is allowed the time it is entitled to for synchronising to a stream
+ * that has just started -- the eDP T7 timing, which mainline polls for in this
+ * same shape -- and the by-hand sequence waits 500 ms before it reads. A retry
+ * would re-drive the link and destroy the question; this only waits.
+ *
+ * The stream is NOT torn down when the sink refuses to synchronise. The write
+ * reports the failure through its exit status and the registers are left where
+ * they are, because the next thing a human does with a black screen is read
+ * them -- and because `video off` is one write away.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_DP_SINK_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS	500
+#define SUN60I_DP_SINK_SYNC_POLL_MS	20
+
+static int sun60i_dp_video_sink_confirms_stream(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	u8 link_status[DP_LINK_STATUS_SIZE];
+	unsigned int waited;
+	int ret;
+
+	for (waited = 0; ; waited += SUN60I_DP_SINK_SYNC_POLL_MS) {
+		msleep(SUN60I_DP_SINK_SYNC_POLL_MS);
+
+		ret = drm_dp_dpcd_read_link_status(&dp->aux, link_status);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(dp->dev,
+				"the stream is enabled but the sink cannot be asked about it: %d\n",
+				ret);
+			return ret;
+		}
+
+		if (link_status[DP_SINK_STATUS - DP_LANE0_1_STATUS] &
+		    DP_RECEIVE_PORT_0_STATUS)
+			break;
+
+		if (waited >= SUN60I_DP_SINK_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS) {
+			dev_err(dp->dev,
+				"the sink does not report itself in sync with the stream after %u ms (DPCD 0x202-0x207 %*ph); the stream is left enabled so the registers can be read\n",
+				waited, DP_LINK_STATUS_SIZE, link_status);
+			return -ENOLINK;
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* Both link predicates again, on the reading that has SINK set: a sink
+	 * can be in sync with a stream it is receiving badly, and a symbol-lock
+	 * or interlane-alignment loss here is a different fault from "no
+	 * picture" and has to be named as itself.
+	 */
